---
display_code: "4.1"
title: Legislation
paper: "1"
order: 1
---

Every digital scenario in the real world sits inside a frame of overlapping
legislation. An online retailer that loses a database of customer addresses
is in scope of one Act. An employee who guesses a colleague's password to
read their email is in scope of another. A company that posts a job advert
worded to discourage older applicants is in scope of a third. Paper 1
rewards students who can read a scenario, recognise which Act bites, and
then explain how it applies. The recognition step is what most students
under-rehearse.

This content area covers the four legislative tiers that the Paper 1 legal
context draws from. Data protection law (the Data Protection Act 2018 and
the UK GDPR) governs how organisations handle personal data about
identifiable living people. The Computer Misuse Act 1990 makes unauthorised
access to computer systems a criminal offence and adds further offences for
damage and intent. The Equality Act 2010 protects nine characteristics from
discrimination at work and in service delivery. Intellectual property law
(principally the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988) protects original
creative and technical work from being copied or exploited without
permission.

Paper 1 legislation questions reward a sequence that maps directly onto the
Name, Explain, Impact frame used across the platform. The Name step is
literal: a wrong Act collapses the answer before the explanation begins. A
student who confuses the Equality Act with the Data Protection Act when the
scenario is about a hiring decision has no route back to the marks. Topics
in this area build the recognition reflex first, then load in the
underlying rules so the Explain step has somewhere to go.

The Method section's worked examples show how a confident legal answer is
structured in practice. Come back to those once you have worked through the
four topics here and feel the recognition step is automatic.
